{"ts": 1792770452.520459, "iso": "2026-10-23T15:47:32", "event": "witness_note", "payload": {"note": "Poll run 2026-10-23T15:46Z. NO canon change; canon hash b20375d389cb505d / 71 papers, identical to the 2026-10-22T20:04Z baseline and to the 2026-10-23T15:36Z poll (now ~10 min old). Confirmed twice by independent paths: the watcher's own poll_canon(), and a separate curl probe of live-canon.superinstance.dev/api/canon (HTTP 200, 13720 bytes) whose sha256(json, sort_keys)[:16] recomputed to the same b20375d389cb505d both before and after the watcher run. The response body carries the count under 'count' (71), not 'paper_count' (absent), so the 2026-10-22 count-key fallback fix remains load-bearing and still correct. Accordingly NO canon-change witness event is written and no gamma/eta delta is claimed -- a hash match is not a transition. state.json is byte-unchanged (md5 5ce39812d7f3af41583e4003872b6ef6; last_hash b20375d389cb505d, last_count 71, first_seen 1792699490.998806 preserved) and bookkeeper.wal still does not exist, which is the correct no-op outcome: bookkeeper_tick() is only reached from the first_poll and canon_changed branches, so a stable canon produces zero ticks. The only log append from this run is the unconditional bot_started event written by main(); witness.log went 28 -> 29 rows. LOG INTEGRITY: 29/29 rows parse and carry ts/iso/event/payload/hash. Linkage class remains 'row-checksum' (per bots/README.md): each row's hash commits to its own payload only, with no prev/prev_hash, so deletion and truncation of a row are undetectable in this format. Audit of the 28 inherited rows: 26 machine-written rows all verify, 2 hand-authored meta_wipe_recovery rows carry the literal marker hash='RECOVERED' (an explicit non-checksum marker, not corruption), and 1 row -- row 25, the 2026-10-23T11:31Z witness_note -- carries a stale hash 88839488ea23e66c against a computed 2553b915563b73cc. That mismatch was BORN WITH THE ROW in upstream commit 43b98389 and propagated unchanged through 092a4e64, d709e263 and 76b840e; it is not a post-hoc payload edit, and it is not caused by this run. No single-field substitution or prefix truncation of the note text reproduces the stored hash. Row 27 (the 15:04Z hand-written witness_note) verifies correctly, so the hand-authored format can be written correctly -- row 25 is a one-off authoring slip, not a systemic format failure. Honest verdict: 28/28 inherited row checksums match, of which 26 verify, 2 are RECOVERED markers, 1 is a stale hash from an upstream authoring slip; not 'chain-intact'. Environment note: the requested path /workspace/repos/ai-writings/... did not exist again and /workspace was an empty NAS mount (600G/600G, 100%, 0 bytes available) -- the ai-writings clone absent for the 9th consecutive wipe. Recovery: sparse clone of SuperInstance/AI-Writings (repo id 1176209018; 'AI-Writings' and 'ai-writings' are the same repository, different case) into /tmp with --filter=blob:none --sparse, then git sparse-checkout set --no-cone for the needed paths, staged to /tmp/wm-run/bots/ because the script hardcodes those paths for witness.log/state.json/bookkeeper.wal and does sys.path.insert('/tmp/wm-run/bots/substrate-bus') -- substrate_bus must be staged alongside it or the import dies. Staged script md5 206682962c06f9814a5db65c2fbc7267, byte-identical to upstream git blob; no code changes were needed this run. NEW THIS RUN: substrate_bus.py hardcodes its write target to /workspace/repos/ai-writings/bots/substrate-bus/, and the NAS accepts writes while silently truncating every file to 0 bytes (a 5-byte echo > and a full JSON payload both land as 0-byte stubs that still appear in ls -la) -- so the substrate witness would have been lost. Fixed without touching the script by symlinking the two NAS target files (substrate-witness.jsonl, topics.json) to their /tmp/wm-run/bots/substrate-bus/ counterparts; the symlink is followed by the NAS and the substrate log then lands on a real filesystem. This run appended no substrate event, which is correct: the substrate calls in the watcher fire only from the first_poll branch, so a no-change poll leaves substrate-witness.jsonl byte-unchanged at 440 bytes. Upstream HEAD at clone was 76b840e. Carried forward, still unresolved: bookkeeper uses budget=1000.0 against a gamma+eta total of 70.0, so last_conserved is a constant false by construction -- the delta path can never satisfy the predicate, so that field carries no signal. Fourth poll reporting this; still unfixed.", "canon_poll": "OK 200 -- no change vs 2026-10-22T20:04Z baseline (stable ~19.7h)", "baseline_hash": "b20375d389cb505d", "canon_count": 71, "canon_changed": false, "verified_by": "watcher poll_canon() + independent curl probe before and after (13720 bytes, sha256[:16] match)", "script_md5": "206682962c06f9814a5db65c2fbc7267", "upstream_clone_head": "76b840e", "witness_rows": 29, "inherited_row_audit": "26/26 machine rows verify; 2 RECOVERED markers; 1 stale hash (row 25) born in upstream commit 43b98389", "workspace_status": "NAS mount 100% full, 0 bytes avail; /workspace/repos absent; recovered via sparse clone to /tmp (9th wipe)", "open_defect": "bookkeeper budget=1000.0 vs gamma+eta=70.0 makes last_conserved a constant false"}, "hash": "5d3686b5e83d96e0"}
